WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:45.560
[Music]

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:52.400
[Sounds of a toy train rolling around a track]

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:57.500
[Boy:] Woo Woo! Woo Woo!

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.160
[Girl:] Faster, faster!

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.140
[Boy:] Can you make it go faster, Grandpa?

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:10.960
[Jim:] Well, we can try, but remember, we've got to 
slow down on the curves.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.020
[Boy:] Can you make it go still faster?

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.920
[Jim:] Still faster. Oh dear.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.200
Fast, faster, the young just have to go fast, don't 
they?

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.380
I should understand that after what happened,

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:39.120
how many years ago was it...40, 43, I guess.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.180
When I was 21 I was ready to take the measure of 
the world.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:49.820
I was working on a surveying crew during my 
vacation from state college.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.320
It was hard work, but I was in a hurry to get ahead. 
The opportunities made me want to stay on

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:03.680
rather than go back to architectural school in the 
fall, and then another thing, there was Jennifer.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:14.680
[Music]

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:23.800
She had tried as well as she could to persuade me 
to finish school, but one day I asked Jenny to 
marry me.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:31.500
I said that I'd work hard, and I promised we'd be 
happy together.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.500
Did I keep that promise? I think I did.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.200
We decided quickly and it wasn't a mistake.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.400
I worked up to the job of field superintendent with 
the company.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:52.520
I remember my first day in charge of the crew, old 
man McGovern saying we'll never bring the job in 
on time.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:56.660
We did.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:04.200
The next year we had a son. Junior looked...I 
guess all babies look that way.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.160
I certainly remember 1917 and '18.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.780
As an army engineer, I had another job that had to 
be done quickly.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:20.020
Then, I returned to Jenny and our son. We were all 
together again.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.800
I worked hard; I spared no effort, least of all my 
own.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.960
Time became a challenge to me; again and again I 
won out over time,

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.200
and finished jobs ahead of schedule.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:43.520
[Music]

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.460
May 14th, 1925 was a day that ended like any 
other work day.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.920
[Steam whistle blows]

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.620
Not an important date on the calendar but an 
important one to me and my family.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:13.100
[Music]

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.120
[Jim collapses]

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.220
[Jenny:] Jim, are you getting ready?

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.500
[Music]

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:31.940
Jim, did you hear me, Jim, what's the matter?

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:37.920
[Jim:] Just feeling tired, very tired.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:44.940
[Jenny:] You've been coming home tired for 
months now. Are you sick, Jim?

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.100
[Jim:] I'll be all right in just a couple minutes.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.700
[Jenny:] I'm getting a doctor.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:59.260
[Jim:] I was wrong, it wasn't a matter of minutes. 
The doctor said I'd be all right in about a year.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:08.200
There was no doubt about it, after all the medical 
tests were made, he said I had tuberculosis.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:15.840
I had to remain in the hospital for treatment, for 
rest, complete rest for about a year.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:26.140
The first six months to be spent on my back, in this 
room resting, wasting time.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:29.880
At night it was even worse.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:33.740
I worried about my family. How would they manage 
without me?

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:41.360
I felt completely helpless.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:47.840
Slowly, too slowly it seemed to me, I began to feel 
better.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:52.100
The weeks and months passed. Then it was 
spring.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:57.740
When I went down for my periodic checkup, the 
doctor showed me that the area of disease

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:02.140
was now quite small.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:08.840
I felt as strong as I had ever felt, why couldn't I be 
discharged now and go back to work?

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.860
The doctor said that the disease was still 
dangerous

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.700
and that it was out of the question for me to leave 
now.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:22.420
Then he told me that I had to give up the idea of 
ever going back to my old job, once and for all.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:26.260
That was a tough blow.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.240
He tried to soften it by letting me go home to visit 
for a few days.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:34.140
Every moment of my freedom was precious.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.300
I didn't even stop to call Jenny.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.640
I wanted to surprise her, wanted to see her face 
when I walked into our home.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:52.580
[Music]

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:57.700
The surprise turned out to be on me.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:03.800
Jenny had been working. That meant little Jimmy 
rushed off to school in the morning,

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.080
not seeing his mother until evening.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:14.620
It wasn't fair to the boy nor to Jenny either, not so 
long as I was able to do a day's work myself.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:21.900
I had to make up for lost time. There was a job to 
be done and I'd been hired to do it.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:29.620
However, it was work for a healthy man and 
whether I'd admitted it or not, I was weak, still sick.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:54.820
[Music]

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:57.600
Once again, I was in the hospital.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.340
I'd tried to hurry.

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:08.340
I'd gone too fast. Now the prescription was at least 
another year, maybe longer.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.880
For days, I felt terribly alone and depressed.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:17.840
Neither the friendly old man who was my new 
roommate nor the nurses could penetrate

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:22.000
the curtain of my helplessness.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:26.320
Yet it was my roommate Pop who finally made me 
realize that I must forget the past

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:29.480
and start over with a new life.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:32.040
Pop loved to grow things.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:37.140
One day in talking about his plants, I think Pop was 
really trying to tell me something.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:43.980
[Pop:] Plants certainly do good here, this begonia 
my sister sent is blooming already.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:48.480
[Jim:] Well maybe this is a fine place for plants, but 
not for me.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:51.060
I can't just lie here.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.000
They say it's a year, suppose it's more?

00:08:55.480 --> 00:09:03.440
[Pop:] I ain't one for wasting time, it's all just a 
question of getting started.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:07.040
[Jim:] What do you mean?

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:11.280
[Pop:] Say, looks like this thing's rooted.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:17.560
Took its own time all right. Some decide to root fast 
and some decide to root slow

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:21.120
and some just can't make up their minds.

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:23.260
It's all according to nature.

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:28.580
[Jim:] I begin to understand what he meant. I 
realized that I too had to grow again.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:38.940
I had to start a second life and I had help, for my 
doctor was all things in one, surgeon, counselor 
and friend.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:44.940
He took me back to my college days, to my three 
years of architectural courses.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:52.000
Now, they suddenly offered the prospect of a new 
life; I had found something to start with, something 
to build on.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:58.540
Soon I was well enough to listen to some sharp 
criticism.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.120
The doctor imitated the way I used to walk and 
then he gave me some sound advice.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.600
I've never forgotten his words.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:10.000
Slow down to normal, slow down to normal.

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:17.820
This became the key to my new life when I left the 
hospital, September 19, 1927.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:20.760
I found a job in a busy architect's office.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:26.620
It was a big step forward and I brought along a 
whole set of new attitudes, including patience.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:33.660
At least one of my coworkers didn't seem to 
understand that I was completely safe to be near.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:38.140
Like a number of people, he didn't realize that your 
doctor won't let you go back to work

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.760
if you're liable to spread TB.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:46.620
Of course, the doctor watched my progress 
closely.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:51.460
In the evenings, Jimmy and I would do our studying 
together.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:57.020
When I took the state licensing exam for an 
architect's license, I really had to pass two tests.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:01.200
In the middle of the exam an old habit came back.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.120
I found myself working faster, hurrying in order to 
finish ahead of schedule.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.900
A moment later I caught myself.

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:18.520
What's your hurry Wilson? I asked. You've got 
plenty of time. Slow down to normal.

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:24.520
I am proud, very proud of that piece of parchment.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:30.800
[Music]

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:36.220
In the next few years I designed all types of 
buildings, little and big.

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:48.220
In 1939, my son Jim got his license and came into 
the office as a junior partner.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:55.560
When Jim returned from the service in 1945 he 
began to take on most of the work, not that I 
couldn't put in a full day myself.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.140
Regular checkups by the doctors proved that my 
health was good,

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:01.900
but now I could afford to take things easier.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:12.680
Then, one morning in 1950 we got an invitation to 
submit a design for an exciting project.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:18.520
Jim realized how much it would mean to me to 
design a TB wing for the new hospital.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:20.420
When do you get started, he asked?

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:22.360
Immediately, I told him.

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:27.440
But, I suppose in a way I got started the day I left 
the hospital 23 years ago.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:30.960
Now, I'd finished.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:36.420
When the job was finally completed it turned out to 
be the most modern tuberculosis hospital

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:38.820
in our part of the country.

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:42.200
It had operating rooms that reflected the latest 
achievements in medical science

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.940
in the fight against tuberculosis.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:49.680
Here in the laboratory, doctors and technicians 
kept abreast of the latest developments

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:51.640
in anti-TB drugs.

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:56.580
Here was an up-to-date x-ray room with the latest 
of equipment.

00:12:56.620 --> 00:13:01.820
In this modern tuberculosis hospital patients had 
an opportunity to retain old skills

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:06.520
and learn new ones to start their second lives.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:11.360
I consider this the greatest achievement of my life,

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:17.680
so the day the hospital opened was the day I 
retired from business.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:23.680
Then there was only one name on the door, that of 
Jim Jr. I didn't have to worry about my son,

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:32.800
I knew he'd do a fine job and besides, I'd been  promising Jenny a second honeymoon.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:39.640
We went back to Niagara Falls of course, but it was 
good to return to our children and grandchildren,

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:49.880
to the quiet excitement of peaceful surroundings, 
to the fullness and expectations of my second life.

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:52.420
[Girl:] Can it go again, Grandpa?

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:55.280
[Boy:] It's not broken. We can fix it, can't we?

00:13:55.320 --> 00:14:02.140
[Jim:] Oh, of course, all you have to do is get it 
back on the track...and slow down to normal.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:25.440
[Music]

